[243.805534, "o", "\r\u001b[2K\r\n\u001b[1;31mmini-swe-agent\u001b[0m\u001b[31m (step \u001b[0m\u001b[1;31m11\u001b[0m\u001b[31m, \u001b[0m\u001b[1;31m$0.00\u001b[0m\u001b[31m):\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m⠼\u001b[0m Waiting for the LM to respond..."]
[243.807487, "o", "\r\u001b[2KThe data shows a strong surface pattern: the correct option is the longest in 64% of training examples. I’ll still train the LoRA properly, but this suggests a \r\ncompact multiple-choice prompt should learn useful priors. First I’ll download GPT-2 and benchmark a forward/backward pass on CPU.\r\n\r\n```\r\ncd /app && python - <<'PY'\r\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\r\nimport time, torch\r\nname='openai-community/gpt2'\r\nt=time.time(); tok=AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name); print('tok loaded',time.time()-t)\r\nt=time.time(); model=AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name); print('model loaded',time.time()-t, sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())/1e6)\r\ntok.pad_token=tok.eos_token\r\ntext='Question: What is hemoglobin?\\nA. Transport oxygen\\nB. Fight infections\\nC. Clot blood\\nD. Digest food\\nAnswer: A'\r\nenc=tok(text, return_tensors='pt')\r\nprint(enc.input_ids.shape, tok.convert_ids_to_tokens(enc.input_ids[0][-5:]))\r\nmodel.train()\r\nt=time.time()\r\nout=model(**enc, labels=enc.input_ids)"]
[243.807516, "o", "\r\nout.loss.backward()\r\nprint('fwd+bwd sec', time.time()-t)\r\nPY\r\n```\r\n\u001b[32m⠼\u001b[0m Waiting for the LM to respond..."]
